@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.Head
@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.LearnPage
@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.SiteUrl
@param page: LearnPage
@param head: Head
@param site: SiteUrl

<%--
    /learn/choc-switches. NO BACKTICKS anywhere in this file -- one closes the raw string
    this page is written in, and jte then reports the error on the wrong line.

    SOURCES, checked 2026-10-03. Kailh's own drawings first:
      - PG1350 (Choc v1), drawing CPG135001D01-12: 15 x 15 mm, total travel 3.0 mm
        (+0/-0.5). PCB layout: center hole 3.4 mm, two post holes 1.9 mm, 11 mm apart, two
        pin holes 1.2 mm at (-5.9, 0) and (-3.8, 5.0) from the center.
      - PG1353 (Choc v2), drawings CPG135301D01 and D03: 15 x 15 mm, cross-shaped stem,
        total travel 3.2 mm +/- 0.25. PCB layout: center hole 5.0 mm, pin holes 1.2 mm at
        (5.9, 0) and (3.8, -5.0), a 2 x 1.5 mm slot, no side-post holes. Kailh draws it
        turned half a turn from PG1350 (the LED window is on the other side); turned the
        same way round, its two pin holes land on PG1350's.
      - PG1232 (Mini Choc): Kailh's own announcement on deskthority (2017) gives 2.4 mm
        total travel and says it is "only smaller than PG1350" and "isn't compatible with
        MX keycap". No Kailh drawing was found, so this page gives none of its dimensions.
        Deskthority's wiki page "Kailh PG1232 series" (checked 2026-10-03) gives its keycap
        mount as "Kailh Choc" and total travel as 2.4 +/- 0.5 mm.
        That its holes differ is read off the footprint the corpus's PG1232 boards place
        (Kailh_PG1232 in davidphilipbarr/36keys, slots rather than round holes).
      - Kailh X is Kailh's PG1425. Nothing more is said of it here.

    THE DIAGRAM IS THE TOP VIEW, the side the switch sits on. Kailh's numbers above have y
    pointing UP; SVG's y points down, so each y is negated when plotted. Checked against
    KiCad footprints, which are drawn from the top: ergogen's choc.js (pins at 0,-5.9 and
    5,-3.8, y down) and ceoloide's switch_choc_v1_v2.js (v2 slot at -5,5.15). Turned a
    quarter turn so the first pin is on the left, the second pin is up-left of center and
    the v2 slot down-right. Plotted with y down unnegated, it was the view from below.
--%>
@template.layout(head = head, site = site, content = @`
    <script type="application/ld+json">$unsafe{page.articleLd}</script>
    <script type="application/ld+json">$unsafe{page.breadcrumbLd}</script>
    <article class="learn">
        <p class="crumb"><a href="/learn">&larr; Learn</a></p>
        <h1>Choc v1, Choc v2 and Mini Choc</h1>

        <p class="learn-answer">
            Choc is Kailh's family of low-profile switches: <strong>Choc v1</strong>,
            <strong>Choc v2</strong> and the smaller <strong>Mini Choc</strong>. A board
            takes the one it was drilled for, so check which before you buy switches.
        </p>

        <figure class="learn-figure">
            <svg class="lf-small" viewBox="0 0 94 38" role="img"
                 aria-label="The PCB holes for Choc v1 and Choc v2, to scale, turned the same way round. Both have two small pin holes in the same places. Choc v1 has a 3.4 mm center hole and two 1.9 mm post holes above and below it. Choc v2 has a 5 mm center hole, no post holes, and a small slot in one corner.">
                <text class="lf-title" x="22" y="6" text-anchor="middle">Choc v1 (PG1350)</text>
                <rect class="lf-cell" x="14.5" y="14.5" width="15" height="15"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="22" cy="22" r="1.7"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="22" cy="16.5" r="0.95"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="22" cy="27.5" r="0.95"/>
                <circle class="lf-mark" cx="16.1" cy="22" r="0.6"/>
                <circle class="lf-mark" cx="18.2" cy="17" r="0.6"/>
                <text class="lf-label" x="22" y="34.5" text-anchor="middle">center hole 3.4 mm</text>

                <text class="lf-title" x="72" y="6" text-anchor="middle">Choc v2 (PG1353)</text>
                <rect class="lf-cell" x="64.5" y="14.5" width="15" height="15"/>
                <circle class="lf-hole" cx="72" cy="22" r="2.5"/>
                <circle class="lf-mark" cx="66.1" cy="22" r="0.6"/>
                <circle class="lf-mark" cx="68.2" cy="17" r="0.6"/>
                <ellipse class="lf-hole" cx="77.15" cy="27" rx="1" ry="0.75"/>
                <text class="lf-label" x="72" y="34.5" text-anchor="middle">center hole 5 mm</text>
            </svg>
            <figcaption>
                The holes each switch needs in the PCB, from Kailh's drawings, to scale and
                seen from the top. The dashed square is the 15 &times; 15 mm switch, the
                colored dots its two pins, in the same place on both. Kailh's v2 drawing also
                has a small 2 &times; 1.5 mm slot.
            </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <table class="learn-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th></th><th>Choc v1</th><th>Choc v2</th><th>Mini Choc</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><th>Kailh part</th><td>PG1350</td><td>PG1353</td><td>PG1232</td></tr>
                <tr><th>Stem</th><td>Two narrow prongs</td><td>A cross, like MX</td><td>Takes Choc caps</td></tr>
                <tr><th>Travel</th><td>3.0 mm</td><td>3.2 mm</td><td>2.4 mm</td></tr>
                <tr><th>PCB holes</th><td>3.4 mm center, two 1.9 mm side posts 11 mm apart</td><td>5 mm center, no side posts</td><td>Its own pattern</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
            A v2 switch won't go in a board drilled only for v1, because its center post
            needs the 5 mm hole. A v1 switch won't go in a v2 board either: its side posts
            have no holes. Some boards are drilled for both, and each design page lists
            every footprint its board takes.
        </p>
        <p>
            The stem decides the keycaps. Most Choc-spaced caps are made for v1 and don't
            fit v2. <a href="/learn/key-spacing">Key spacing</a> names some that do.
        </p>
        <p>
            The Mini Choc fits only Mini Choc boards. Kailh says it doesn't take MX caps,
            and Deskthority's switch wiki lists its keycap mount as Kailh Choc, the same as
            Choc v1's.
        </p>

        @template.learn.list(list = page.list("v1"))
        @template.learn.list(list = page.list("v2"))
        @template.learn.list(list = page.list("mini"))
        <p>
            There is one more low-profile Kailh switch here, the Kailh X (PG1425), a
            different design again.
        </p>
        @template.learn.list(list = page.list("x"))
    </article>
`)
